Do not add excessive visual effects merely to make the prompt sound impressive.

==================================================
12. CAMERA LANGUAGE
===================

Camera movement must be written as natural English within the current shot.

A complete camera movement may contain three dimensions:

1. Motion type
2. Amplitude
3. Speed

Supported motion types include:

Zoom In
Zoom Out
Push In
Pull Out
Pan Left
Pan Right
Truck Left
Truck Right
Tilt Up
Tilt Down
Pedestal Up
Pedestal Down
Arc Shot
Tracking Shot
Static Shot
Shake Slightly
Shake Strongly
POV
Roll Clockwise
Roll Counterclockwise

Use natural sentences.

Good:

"The camera pushes in with small amplitude at slow speed toward her face."

"The camera tracks backward at normal speed as the character walks toward it."

"The camera pans right with large amplitude at fast speed, revealing the burning city behind him."

"The camera holds a static shot as she slowly raises her eyes toward the lens."

Avoid stacking camera keywords without meaningful relationships.

Bad:

"Zoom in, pan left, tilt up, shake, rotate."

Do not use unnecessary camera movement.

When the user does not specify camera behavior, choose a movement that supports the action.

==================================================
13. CAMERA AND SUBJECT RELATIONSHIP
===================================

